<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">What you should be able to say by the end</div>
  <p class="cu-statement">A hazard becomes a disaster through the choices a society makes about where and how it builds.</p>
  <p class="gq-label">Questions that get you there</p>
  <ul class="guiding-questions">
    <li><span class="gq-type">Factual</span> Which hazards does each plate boundary type produce?</li>
    <li><span class="gq-type">Conceptual</span> Why do the same buildings and the same technologies produce different outcomes at different boundaries?</li>
    <li><span class="gq-type">Debatable</span> With a fixed budget, which losses is it acceptable to design for rather than prevent?</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="prose">
  <p>This year Unit 3 is a content unit: Earth's layers and what moves the plates, the four settings (convergent, divergent, transform, hotspot), and why each setting produces the hazards it does. Most of it is done at home, in two take-homes across three pages. Each page works the same way: read it or watch it, then everyone does the same task. Two self-checks in Google Classroom are not graded and show you the answers.</p>
  <p>The unit is paired with primary-source case studies: <a href="/reference/case-studies/tohoku-2011/">Tōhoku 2011</a>, Kobe 1995, Sumatra-Andaman 2004, Pinatubo 1991, Pompeii 79 CE. You don't memorize these. You use one of them as the evidence for your poster. Browse the full <a href="/reference/case-studies/">Case Study Library</a>.</p>
</div>

